# Adaptive ZIP file read-ahead experiment

Hypothesis: the fixed 8 KiB buffer reduced tiny-file amplification, but large
streaming entries now issue many small physical reads. The ZIP crate wraps its
compressed input in a standard BufReader, whose requests can be 8 KiB. Increasing
read-ahead only after sustained sequential reads may recover large-entry speed
without reintroducing the original small-file read amplification.

The isolated candidate starts at 8 KiB, promotes to 256 KiB after 64 KiB of
sequential reads when the next request is at least 8 KiB, and resets to 8 KiB on
actual seek movement. A clone starts small at the logical position. Cached
immutable archives are unchanged. Buffer resizing preserves the unread logical
position; added tests compare bytes across growth, seeks, clones and invalid
seeks. The all-platform prototype is rejected on Linux; the retained candidate applies adaptation only on Windows.

`.github/workflows/zip-adaptive-input.yml` builds before/after binaries and on
Windows also compares the checksum-pinned actual alpha executable. All readers
use normal memory budgets and four workers. Seven interleaved measured rounds,
warmup excluded, with direct byte/tree verification after every extraction.

## Results

[Run 40989907784](https://github.com/marlboro-red/zipper/actions/runs/40989907784)
used source `a3ca8d9` plus `candidate.patch`. The historical workflow must run
at that baseline, before the implementation is merged. Windows uses static CRT
and Rust 1.96.0, matching the published alpha build settings. These are local
filesystem, warm-cache process timings, not durable disk completion or cold-cache
results. Default compatibility-backend extraction is outside this experiment.

Windows random-entry streaming extraction is the clear improvement: 37.02 to
34.66 ms (6.4% less time), with disjoint observed ranges. Peak working set rises
from 3.88 to 4.10 MiB. Its median returns within the actual release's range.
Mixed and native-fast fallback medians improve, but overlapping ranges make
those inconclusive. Flat tiny-file native extraction has a worse median with
overlapping ranges; this is not evidence of a universal win.

Linux random streaming extraction regresses 39.89 to 46.57 ms and mixed extraction
123.41 to 127.49 ms with disjoint ranges. Keep fixed 8 KiB buffers there. Mac
mixed, flat and real-dependency tree ranges overlap; keep its existing reader too.
Avoiding extra buffer copies appears more valuable on Linux; this is a hypothesis,
not a measured syscall or CPU profile diagnosis.

All samples, archive hashes/sizes, binary hashes, order and byte verification are
retained alongside this report. Each table cell is median [min, max] milliseconds;
memory is maximum observed process peak MiB. Same archive for every engine.

